#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229651 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b32f48-aea6-5ccd-94e9-47141767a803" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a5c777-0ee7-596b-bfcd-3edec0a59b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228328} {:answer/id #uuid "0be9925c-0108-5108-97b3-548898da4f79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228329} {:answer/id #uuid "16b32ced-43e7-573f-be4d-9e774ca1bc40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228330} {:answer/id #uuid "40ba967a-e213-5503-b1a3-9bb8a7fdf416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228331} {:answer/id #uuid "4b7ff7ca-d740-5968-b55f-04b6afb9efa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16b32ced-43e7-573f-be4d-9e774ca1bc40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228330} :answer-field/id #uuid "9758f7f5-28c0-5670-b564-3f046bf0db4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f75fdde8-1a31-5115-9dda-a22cf62a4bb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-336636" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/assets/497263348010d38f2372e818b89ee2294d8f207366521f2bddce4a0628f457b1.png)\n\nConsider the RC circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{P}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{Q}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.01\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.01}=100q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+100q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+5q=\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+5{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-5t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+5(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +5\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(5\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +5\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 5\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{145},\\beta =\\frac{1}{29}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-5t}-\\frac{2}{145}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{29}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=-2,Q=1,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=-2+1=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b88fec8-167e-5900-9ae8-109affedf08c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a55d7775-20ce-52e6-8f78-ce87d1e3634e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.009" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337761} {:answer/id #uuid "92e84292-26d1-5bbc-a499-3ecfc3a8ad9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.028" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337762} {:answer/id #uuid "0922c13a-c0f4-5cf8-aac0-31e70ec2eeef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.051" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337763} {:answer/id #uuid "adb24988-447c-5909-9b53-9a4847792366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.036" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337764} {:answer/id #uuid "5f5cf79a-2957-5422-a9d5-01f81b2bc0b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adb24988-447c-5909-9b53-9a4847792366" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.036" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337764} :answer-field/id #uuid "73f91cb2-71f2-55d3-ba55-a7b59c2d58f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d40b0a8b-0088-5197-95b5-05c19f647186" :question/math-academy-id "q-337129" :question/problem "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.005\\,\\text{F}.$ Find, in coulombs rounded to three decimal places, the circuit capacitor's charge after $0.05$ seconds, given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{50}$ coulombs.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.005}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+10{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+10\\alpha =\\frac{3}{5}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{3}{50}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{50}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{3}{50}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{50}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{50},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{50} \\\\ A{e}^{-10(0)}+\\frac{3}{50} & =\\frac{1}{50} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{1}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\nq(t)=\\frac{3}{50}-\\frac{1}{25}{e}^{-10t}\n$$\n\nFinally, the charge on the capacitor in the circuit after $t=0.05$ seconds, in coulombs, is\n\n$$\nq(0.05)=\\frac{3}{50}-\\frac{1}{25}{e}^{-10(0.05)}≈0.036,\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b88fec8-167e-5900-9ae8-109affedf08c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d97df48-cfd9-5377-bdfb-97463a12a4a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.019" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337768} {:answer/id #uuid "68207ec8-b59b-5565-9758-cf2244add9f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.048" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337769} {:answer/id #uuid "58b3c924-491d-50f3-b073-80481fee03eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337770} {:answer/id #uuid "82970af8-81f6-568a-bbb6-895d86999ac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.033" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337771} {:answer/id #uuid "0bcedb0f-423a-5d38-a360-da9754b7fcce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.037" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82970af8-81f6-568a-bbb6-895d86999ac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.033" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337771} :answer-field/id #uuid "e206b594-9848-556d-8315-7bb831b067e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f9ba34bd-9215-5637-ab3a-01c4e3ac1afc" :question/math-academy-id "q-337130" :question/problem "A series RC circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $24\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $75\\,Ω,$ and a capacitance of $0.002\\,\\text{F}.$ Find, in coulombs rounded to three decimal places, the circuit capacitor's charge after $0.06$ seconds, given that the initial charge is $\\frac{1}{40}$ coulombs.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=75\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=75I=75\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.002}=500q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n24=75\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+500q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{20}{3}q=\\frac{8}{25}.\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{20}{3}{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-20t/3},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{20}{3}\\alpha =\\frac{8}{25}\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{6}{125}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{125}.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-20t/3}+\\frac{6}{125}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{40}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $q(0)=\\frac{1}{40},$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(0) & =\\frac{1}{40} \\\\ A{e}^{-20(0)/3}+\\frac{6}{125} & =\\frac{1}{40} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{23}{1000}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $q(t):$\n\n$$\nq(t)=\\frac{6}{125}-\\frac{23}{1000}{e}^{-20t/3}\n$$\n\nFinally, the charge on the capacitor in the circuit after $t=0.06$ seconds, in coulombs, is\n\n$$\nq(0.06)=\\frac{6}{125}-\\frac{23}{1000}{e}^{-20(0.06)/3}≈0.033,\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b32f48-aea6-5ccd-94e9-47141767a803" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc39bafe-661c-5e6f-ab41-8f3b41f1e784" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337775} {:answer/id #uuid "ad364252-2438-5d4a-a82f-2bd291cedc93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337776} {:answer/id #uuid "ad6ba30f-a5cc-5363-98ce-3ff529ab230c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337777} {:answer/id #uuid "c04d8a5a-3a87-5258-acdc-c8b9cd6f7e32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337778} {:answer/id #uuid "9fae3bcd-5f7b-54cd-9b94-dca3bd4aed67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad6ba30f-a5cc-5363-98ce-3ff529ab230c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337777} :answer-field/id #uuid "7128afa2-5873-5a81-b5c6-a498ef35de3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b15d2366-1b26-5831-ae19-4bdf4f17a47c" :question/math-academy-id "q-337140" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/assets/db924dbe346c279e307d0b75b29cbccbfab566c251f90e3a63024264d5a512e5.png)\n\nConsider the RC circuit shown above. Given that the charge $q(t)$ of the capacitor at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nq(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{P}{425}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{Q}{425}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $100\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor of capacitance $0.0025\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=100\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=100I=100\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{0.0025}=400q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)=100\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+400q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+4q=\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+4{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-4t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ (-\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+(4\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =\\frac{3}{25}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-\\alpha +4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) \\\\ 4\\alpha +\\beta =\\frac{3}{25}\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{12}{425},\\beta =\\frac{3}{425}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{12}{425}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{3}{425}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-4t}+\\frac{12}{425}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\frac{3}{425}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=12,Q=3,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=12+3=15.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186337780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96b32f48-aea6-5ccd-94e9-47141767a803" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RC Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b405b472-4bbd-520a-98f7-341dc6ad50a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337782} {:answer/id #uuid "28fec066-9118-5c75-b4ce-69ebbc5b093f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337783} {:answer/id #uuid "b2aebcbc-b7cf-5420-9334-ffca902b40c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337784} {:answer/id #uuid "52a316a9-f222-563a-bca9-dacf638dc834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337785} {:answer/id #uuid "db435df1-8a8e-5b4e-8da2-aad9cc871490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52a316a9-f222-563a-bca9-dacf638dc834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337785} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab5794af-f53d-52d3-8d8c-9a0f4d6609e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bee68d03-ab65-535d-8f7a-706c85ffce90" :question/math-academy-id "q-337351" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059356/assets/e37bc2504bb5c95ef5624760ac1b4639c0ff02aeb79bd5dccb1e7257215e3b3c.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RC circuit shown above, in coulombs, rounded to two decimal places. \n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RC circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $40\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor with resistance $20\\,Ω,$ and a capacitor with capacitance $\\frac{1}{200}\\,\\text{F}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs. \n\n$$\nI=\\frac{\\text{d}q}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RC circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt},\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{(\\frac{1}{200})}=200q.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n40\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=20\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+200q,\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}+10q=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{q}_{c}}{dt}+10{q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{q}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+10(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +10\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(10\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +10\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 10\\alpha +2\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{5}{26},\\beta =\\frac{1}{26}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t)={q}_{c}(t)+{q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}+\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-10t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nq(t)≈{q}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{5}{26})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{26})}^{2}}≈0.20,\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}]}
